! cm_adjacent_offsets.strap -- THE LOOK-AHEAD'S STORE-FETCH INTERLOCK,
! for CONNECT TO MEMORY.  Thirteen blocks of two one-bit CMs into one
! word, and whether the second preserves the first.
!
! >>> THIS DECK FOUND A REAL MODEL BUG AND IS NOW ITS REGRESSION
! TEST.  storeTarget() in StretchIBox.bsv listed the floating-point
! stores and the four VFL store codes and had NO CASE for the
! connectives, so CONNECT TO MEMORY -- which writes storage -- was
! invisible to the look-ahead's level-to-level storeAddr interlock.
! A younger level fetching the word a CM was about to write took its
! copy BEFORE the write, and because CM's store is a read-modify-
! write that reuses the fetched word, the stale copy went straight
! back -- undoing the CM before it. <<<
!
! WHERE IT CAME FROM.  Printed p.19 lines 9-11 set three single bits
! in the channel status table entry in a row:
!
!     CM1111(BU,1),.61($3)      -SET UNIT COUNT TO TWO
!     CM1111(BU,1),.27($3)      -MULIT BIT ON IN CST
!     CM0000(BU,1),.29($3)      -MEANINGLESS ASG BIT OFF
!
! and running a DISK card through pp.17-19 left bit 27 set and bit 61
! LOST.  Halts planted between the rows showed bit 61 present after
! line 9 and gone after line 10.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE BLOCKS ARE FOR -- and the two theories they killed
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   D1  .61 then .27            D7  .27 then .29
!   D2  .27 then .61            D8  .29 then .31
!   D3  .29 then .27  <- failed D9  .29, NOP, .27
!   D4  .61 then .62            DA  .61 then .59
!   D5  .29 alone               DB  .29 then .3
!   D6  .27 alone               DC  NOP, .29, .27
!                               DD  .29 then .27  <- failed
!
! "THE WRITE IS EIGHT BITS WIDE, not one" fits D3 -- a write at 27
! covering 27-34 would take out 29 -- and every block through D8.
! DA KILLS IT: a write at 59 covering 59-66 would take out 61, and
! does not.
!
! "THE SECOND WRITE READS A STALE COPY" fits D3 and D9, and D1, D7,
! D8, DA and DB ALL KILL IT: every one is a second write into a word
! the instruction before it just changed, and every one keeps both
! bits.  The staleness is real but it is not unconditional -- it
! happens only when the LOOK-AHEAD supplied the operand.
!
! >>> WHAT IDENTIFIED IT AS STATE RATHER THAN ARITHMETIC IS DC
! AGAINST DD.  Those two blocks are THE SAME INSTRUCTION PAIR ON THE
! SAME DATA, one after the other, differing only in what precedes
! them -- and DC kept both bits while DD lost one.  Two identical
! instructions on identical operands cannot correctly give two
! different answers. <<<
!
! The offsets were never the variable: 29-then-27 failed while
! 61-then-59, the same descent two bits apart in the other half of
! the word, passed.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT BEFORE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Printed p.18 writes the same kind of pair at .29 and .27 and comes
! out right, because its first write is CM0000 -- it CLEARS bit 29,
! so losing that write and re-clearing the bit are the same picture.
! Every other deck in the corpus writes a count field LAST.  p.19 is
! the first program here that sets a high-offset bit and then goes on
! writing lower ones into the same word.
!
! THE INJECTION IS THE FIX ITSELF: removing storeTarget()'s
! Connective case puts D3 and DD back to one bit each and leaves the
! other eleven blocks passing.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

START   CM1111(BU,1), D1+.61
        CM1111(BU,1), D1+.27

        CM1111(BU,1), D2+.27
        CM1111(BU,1), D2+.61

        CM1111(BU,1), D3+.29
        CM1111(BU,1), D3+.27

        CM1111(BU,1), D4+.61
        CM1111(BU,1), D4+.62

        CM1111(BU,1), D5+.29

        CM1111(BU,1), D6+.27

        CM1111(BU,1), D7+.27
        CM1111(BU,1), D7+.29

        CM1111(BU,1), D8+.29
        CM1111(BU,1), D8+.31

! >>> THE DECISIVE PAIR: the same writes as D3, with an unrelated
! instruction between them.  If D9 holds both bits and D3 does not,
! the second write is reading a STALE copy of the word; if D9 fails
! the same way, the write is simply WIDER than one bit. <<<
        CM1111(BU,1), D9+.29
        NOP, 0
        CM1111(BU,1), D9+.27

! Descending within the LOW half, close together.
        CM1111(BU,1), DA+.61
        CM1111(BU,1), DA+.59

! Descending within the HIGH half, far apart.
        CM1111(BU,1), DB+.29
        CM1111(BU,1), DB+.3

! The SAME pair as D3, shifted by one half-word in the instruction
! stream.  If this one holds both bits, the fault is not about the
! offsets at all.
        NOP, 0
        CM1111(BU,1), DC+.29
        CM1111(BU,1), DC+.27

! And the D3 pair again, unshifted, to show it is repeatable.
        CM1111(BU,1), DD+.29
        CM1111(BU,1), DD+.27

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! words below would straddle.
        SLC, (8)2040.0
D1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D6      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D7      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D8      DD(BU,64,8), 0
D9      DD(BU,64,8), 0
DA      DD(BU,64,8), 0
DB      DD(BU,64,8), 0
DC      DD(BU,64,8), 0
DD      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
